{
  "scores": {
    "spectacle": 42,
    "readability": 88,
    "choreography": 72,
    "craft": 85,
    "originality": 55
  },
  "evidence": "The timeline shows clean word-by-word handoffs with no clipping on held frames (e.g., 'the water' at 2.0s/2.65s, 'hold me close' at 3.6s/4.15s, 'do not let' at 6.75s/7.35s). The large held frame and portrait frame both present centered, high-contrast serif text on a dark navy field with generous margins and no collisions. The light-background frame confirms the text remains legible when inverted to a pale field, though the contrast is softer there. Transitions appear as simple fades (visible in the 4.70s and 5.45s frames where text is mid-fade), which are smooth but unremarkable. The frame-difference metrics (mean ~0.001, max ~0.006 at 12fps) support gentle, low-motion fades rather than any dynamic event. There is no scale shift, depth, transformation, or reveal — the visual event is limited to text appearing and disappearing. Typography is well-set (consistent tracking, appropriate size, clean serif), and composition is balanced in both orientations. The main weakness is the absence of any memorable visual payoff or distinctive concept; it reads as a competent but generic lyric card sequence.",
  "critical_issues": [
    "No striking visual event or memorable payoff — the scene is a plain fade-in/out of text with no transformation, depth, or reveal, which caps Spectacle well below the 95 threshold.",
    "The light-background frame, while legible, shows noticeably reduced contrast compared to the dark frames, suggesting the color relationship is not fully optimized for both backgrounds."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Introduce a single purposeful visual event tied to the lyric — e.g., a subtle water-reflection shimmer on 'the water', a slow scale or blur transition on 'hold me close', or a light-source shift on 'golden light' — to create a memorable payoff without adding clutter.",
    "Add a brief anticipation beat before each phrase (e.g., a 1–2 frame dim or slight letter-spacing ease-in) and a more deliberate finish (e.g., a gentle settle or micro-zoom-out) to strengthen Choreography beyond a plain fade.",
    "Tighten the light-background contrast by darkening the text slightly or adding a very subtle warm tint to the pale field so legibility matches the dark-frame performance.",
    "Consider a single distinctive typographic or compositional gesture (e.g., a hairline rule, a soft vignette, or a slight baseline shift on the final phrase 'do not let go') to elevate Originality and Craft beyond a standard centered-text layout."
  ]
}
